  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Write ${r}^{6}+4$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${r}^{6}+4=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n{r}^{6}+4={({r}^{3})}^{2}+{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{6}+4 & ={r}^{6}-(-4) \\\\  & ={r}^{6}-(4{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={({r}^{3})}^{2}-{(2i)}^{2} \\\\  & =({r}^{3}+2i)({r}^{3}-2i)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
